# Source is LF, on every platform, in the working tree as well as the index.
#
# Not a style preference. This project exists to preserve a file's line endings
# byte for byte, and its own sources flipping between CRLF and LF -- which is
# what happens the first time any tool rewrites one on Windows -- turns a
# three-line change into a whole-file diff that nobody can review. It happened
# here, twice, before this file existed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The corpus is evidence, not source.
#
# Its bytes are the thing under test: P1.9 builds BOM, CRLF and mixed-ending
# variants from these files, and the round-trip tests assert byte-for-byte
# equality against them. Letting git normalise line endings on the way in or
# out would quietly rewrite the fixtures and the tests would still pass --
# against a corpus that no longer resembles what translators actually have.
corpus/** -text
*.usfm -text
*.sfm -text
*.SFM -text
